The 1932–33 Scottish football season was [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]]'s 45th season of competitive football, in which they competed in the [[Scottish First Division]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.londonhearts.com/scores/a1930/celtic1933.htm|title=Results for Celtic for 1932-33|website=www.londonhearts.com}}</ref> and the [[Scottish Cup]].

Celtic ended the league in fourth position below [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]], [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] and champions [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]], thus failing to win the league for the seventh season in a row, which was their longest dry spell ever at the time.

However, Celtic managed to win the Scottish Cup for the 14th time in their history, their 31st major domestic honour. After defeating [[Dunfermline Athletic F.C.|Dunfermline Athletic]] 1-7 away in the first round and both [[Falkirk F.C.|Falkirk]] and [[Partick Thistle F.C.|Partick Thistle]] at home, they needed replays to advance against both [[Albion Rovers F.C.|Albion Rovers]] in the fourth round and [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] in the semi-final.

Facing [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] in the final, the same rival they had faced in [[1931 Scottish Cup Final|the 1931 final]], they beat them 1-0 at [[Hampden Park]] with a [[Jimmy McGrory]] goal, in front of over 100,000 spectators.

